{"type":"item.started","item":{"id":"item_6","type":"command_execution","command":"/bin/bash -c \"cd /home/nitro/Desktop/consensus-assurance/runs/2026-10-14_09-22-37-hashicorp_raft-real-run/draft; python3 - <<'EOF'\nimport json\np='update.json'\nd=json.load(open(p))\ni=d['interpretations'][0]\ni['answers']={\n \\\"9c549c026d7341f2b1e328826eac6dc3-gap-0\\\": i['answers'][\\\"9c549c026d7341f2b1e328826eac6dc3-gap-0\\\"],\n \\\"a8b8d696ac934beda1434a93b5d7758f-gap-1\\\": {\n   \\\"explanation\\\":\\\"Partly answered by source and receipt trace, not yet executed. The obvious route cannot work: a voter that never receives the silently-created {A,B,E} entry is strictly behind in the log, and the log up-to-date check in requestVote/requestPreVote (raft.go 1731-1746, 1812-1828) makes any voter that holds the entry refuse to vote for it, so the removed voter C cannot win alone or with a lagging peer. A disjoint-majority election therefore needs a majority of the OLD configuration whose members all lack the entry, which means the entry must commit without one of the old voters acking it. That is reachable because appendConfigurationEntry installs the new configuration into the commitment immediately on append (raft.go 1238-1240), so {A,B,E} commits with A plus a promoted, already-caught-up nonvoter E, without B. The remaining question is whether the partitioned old voter B can then be elected by the old majority {B,C} in a higher term and truncate A's committed entry X when the partition heals (appendEntries clears the conflicting suffix, raft.go 1541-1552), which would lose a committed entry. Requirements identified: a stale-config leader A that keeps leadership long enough, a caught-up nonvoter E (so the change commits without B), B partitioned for the change, and healing afterwards so the higher-term leader's appendEntries reaches A. The next experiment is designed to that discriminator.\\\",\n   \\\"resolved\\\": False}}\nd['gaps']=[{\n \\\"investigation_id\\\":\\\"9c549c026d7341f2b1e328826eac6dc3-question-0\\\",\n \\\"question\\\":\\\"Can the silently-created two-server change ({A,B,C} -> {A,B,E}, committed with A plus a promoted caught-up nonvoter E while B is partitioned) let the old majority {B,C} elect a leader in a higher term that then truncates A's committed entry X when the partition heals, and can the two sides therefore hold leaders of different terms over the same log?\\\"}]\njson.dump(d, open(p,'w'), indent=2)\nEOF\ncd /home/nitro/Desktop/consensus-assurance/runs/2026-10-14_09-22-37-hashicorp_raft-real-run; /home/nitro/Desktop/consensus-assurance/.venv/bin/python -I -B -c \\\"import sys; sys.path.insert(0, '/home/nitro/Desktop/consensus-assurance/src'); from consensus_assurance.cli import main; raise SystemExit(main())\\\" validate --run . --submission update.json 2>&1 | head -3\"","aggregated_output":"","exit_code":null,"status":"in_progress"}}
